  "body": "Stacked on #3471 (merge #3471 first).\n\n## What changed\n- `arcFateOf` (`crates/post/src/post/arc_guard.rs`) has one more rule. The written start and the written end differ, but they are on one ray from the written centre (the same angle). Or one end is on the written centre. If the record turns half a circle or less, the answer is `\"linear\"`. The post writes its straight feed move.\n- The rule reads the numbers of the written words. It uses only the zero of the cross product and the sign of the dot product. A mirrored axis or a lathe diameter word does not change these.\n- A record whose own ends in the stream are on one ray (stream sweep 0) stays an arc. `emitArcWithRadius` reads that as a full turn.\n- `fanuc-lathe.cps` and `tormach-lathe.cps` now write the feed move for `\"linear\"` in their meridian arc path. Before, they wrote no block for any answer that was not `\"arc\"`.\n- The old inline sweep code moved into the helper `_arcSweep`. Its behaviour did not change.\n- Decision: `docs/decisions/3474-same-angle-arc.md`.\n\n## Cause\nThe #3442 guard compares only the written ends. When the ends differ only along the radius, the guard returned `\"arc\"`. On the parent branch every mill post wrote such a block. Examples:\n- Fanuc mm: `G01 X10.011 Y5.` then `G03 X10.01 Y5. Z5.5 I-0.011 J0. F180`.\n- LinuxCNC inch: `G1 X0.4011 Y0.2` then `G3 X0.401 Y0.2 Z0.2165 I-0.0011 J0 F7.09`.\n\nThe start and the end are at the same angle about the centre. The radii are 0.011 and 0.010. RS-274 / ISO 6983 reads an arc whose end is at its start angle as a full turn. No control ran these blocks. A straight move is correct for each reading.\n\n## Tests\nNew file `crates/post/tests/same_angle_arcs.rs`:\n- `fanuc_and_linuxcnc_post_a_same_angle_arc_as_a_straight_move`: Fanuc and LinuxCNC, mm and inch. No arc block, and a straight move to the written end.\n- `every_post_writes_a_same_angle_arc_as_its_straight_move`: all 14 mill posts in each unit that they offer, and the two lathes. The output is byte-equal to the straight feed to the same end. TinyG writes 4 decimals in mm. There the mm witness is a visible arc, and the test asserts that it stays one arc.\n- `arc_fate_reads_a_same_angle_short_arc_as_a_straight_move`: the rule. Same angle, end on centre, a long CW record, an opposite end, a normal arc, and a stream sweep of zero.\n\nAll three were red on the parent head (#3471, `40e1baae7`).\n\nMutations (each put back, then restored):\n- The guard of the parent PR: all 3 pins red.\n- The `fanuc-lathe.cps` call site of the parent PR: `every_post_...` red (`fanuc-lathe mm`).\n- The `tormach-lathe.cps` call site of the parent PR: `every_post_...` red (`tormach-lathe mm`).\n- No `sweep > 1e-9` term: `radius_arcs::near_180_and_full_circle_arcs_in_all_planes_hold_chord_and_center` and `arc_fate_...` red.\n\nGate: `cargo xtask gate quick --base base-3471` (the parent head): 75 steps, 0 failed. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Goldens moved\nNone.\n\n## Not exercised\n- No control ran the blocks. The reading of the controls is from RS-274 / ISO 6983, not from a run.\n- A written end behind the written start (a written sweep of nearly a full turn on a short record). We found no input that makes it. It is recorded as dropped in the decision.\n- The rule uses the written absolute centre word. The control's centre is the start plus the written `I J`. The two can differ by one output step.\n- Mill-turn, plasma and wire posts get the rule through the same `arcFate` call. They have no witness in the new file.\n\n## Overlaps\n- Stacked on #3471: it adds `arc_guard.rs` and `arcFateOf`, which this PR changes. Retarget this PR to `main` before #3471 merges.\n\nChangelog: A post now writes a straight move for a very small arc whose written start and end have the same angle about the centre. Before, a control could read the block as a full turn.\n\nFixes #3474\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
